[b]v0.1.0:[/b]
[h1]Flavorium Universalis - Rhomania - First Release[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.3.* | Requires: Flavorium Universalis and the Fate of the Phoenix content pack[/i]

[hr][/hr]

[i]Early Alpha preview. The mod is not fully tested yet. The western province situation and exarchate content, along with the Indian side of the mission tree, are untested, and balance will change.[/i]

First release. A Byzantine flavour submod that exists because of one criticism of Fate of the Phoenix we think is correct: every unique reward in it sits on the Latin side of the Latinitas and Rhomanismos slider, and the only route to the Roman tag force-moves your capital to Rome. A Greek-speaking Orthodox empire in 1337 is therefore pushed toward re-Latinising itself and abandoning Constantinople.

[h2]Taxis against Dynatoi[/h2]

A second societal axis for the empire's internal politics, which decided more than the language question did. Taxis is the fisc, the bureaux and the salaried official; Dynatoi are the provincial magnates and the families who supply the soldiers. Crossing it with the existing Latin and Greek axis gives four distinct endgames.

A parallel Greek advance line answers the Latin advances point for point, and the tagmata give a heavy infantry line to set against the legionaries. Both are gated on the Rhomanismos side, so pushing Latin costs you access to them.

[h2]Rome, governed from Constantinople[/h2]

Appoint an exarch from your own court to a seat in the recovered west, on one of three terms, governing as your subject while the capital stays where the empire is. Four seats, one per late-Roman praetorian prefecture: Ravenna, Carthage, Spania and the Gauls.

[list]
[*] [b]The arc opens on its own[/b] once enough of a region is held, with an event that nominates a man from your council.
[*] [b]Endings are accumulated rather than chosen,[/b] from a hereditary exarchal dynasty to a fully absorbed province, decided by a loyalty score that has been moving since the appointment.
[*] [b]Appetite is tracked separately from loyalty.[/b] He can be perfectly loyal and still want the Romagna, and the better answers are gated on your ruler or your cabinet.
[*] [b]Each province fails in its own way,[/b] with its own thread: an African frontier that never settles, a Spanish strip that cannot feed its garrison, and a Gaul that does not regard itself as having been recovered.
[*] [b]The papacy is a standing quarrel with no resolution,[/b] including an excommunication that costs an Orthodox emperor little and his Catholic-governing exarch a great deal.
[/list]

[h2]The West[/h2]

A situation that opens with your first exarch. Imperial grip and western autonomy are tracked separately rather than as two ends of one bar, because both were high at once historically.

The prefectures form a consistory, dormant until you convene it. Votes are weighted three for the Augustus, two for the Senior Exarch and one for each other exarch, so one prefecture cannot outvote you, two produce a tie, and three carry the chamber. It turns against you because you succeeded.

The consistory votes on the four powers a prefecture actually exercised: the western armies, the revenue, the rite, and how a dead prefect is replaced. A west that votes itself local usage drifts Latin, and the drift lands on the empire.

Seven repeatable actions run alongside, available with or without mission packs. Six are decisions; whether a summoned exarch actually comes is not, and the odds read off a score the other six have been moving for decades. The West Is Restive now appears on your modifier list when a rising is one step away, with a dispatch explaining what changed.

[h2]Also included[/h2]

[list]
[*] The church union and its consequences.
[*] The Justinianic reconquest as a mission path.
[*] A Mamluk confrontation in the Levant that reads the map rather than assuming the 1337 start.
[*] Eleven Byzantine bureaucracies extended with Taxis and Dynatoi drift that scales with funding.
[*] French, German and Spanish localization throughout.
[/list]
